        {
          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "That's the decisive argument, and it's a product one — which is why the peer-convergence framing wasn't getting at it.\n\nThe shipped spec already requires the UI to render **the result**, not just the call:\n\n> The web chat SHALL render tool activity inline in the message stream — the call (tool name + arguments summary), a running state, and the result (or error)\n\nSo the user is looking at the tool output on screen. The model isn't. They are reading the same conversation with different information, and the user has no way to know that. \"What was the second result?\" is not an edge case — for a search tool it's the normal next turn. The model either hallucinates or silently re-runs the search and gets different hits.\n\nThat's a worse failure than losing agentic continuity, because it's *invisible*. The user's mental model is \"we're both looking at this.\"\n\nThree things follow, and one of them is a correction to my own plan:\n\n**The eval measures the wrong thing.** Task 1.1 asks \"does the next turn still answer correctly\" — model-centric, model-graded, needs credentials. The real question is \"can the user reference output they can see,\" and that's *deterministically demonstrable*: tool returns five hits, assistant text names two, user asks about the third, assert the request carries nothing about the third. That's the test I already have as 3.5. The eval was never going to settle this, and this doesn't need settling.\n\n**\"No consumer until #215\" was wrong.** I used that to defer the projection. But the consumer is `search_conversations`, shipped, today, plus a user asking a follow-up. #215 widens the payloads; it doesn't create the need.\n\n**The boundary requirement is now pinning a defect.** It reads as \"raw persisted tool results SHALL NOT be replayed.\" Technically a fenced projection still satisfies it — a projection isn't the raw result — but the framing asserts current behavior is the contract, which is backwards if we've concluded current behavior is wrong.\n\nWhat I'd change, and I'd rather you push back on the scope than the direction:\n\nRewrite that requirement to state the **durable invariants** instead of the current behavior — what must never reach a later turn regardless (provider-native tool blocks, provider-native reasoning, credentials, unrelated tool payloads), plus the properties any projection must have (fenced, labelled untrusted, bounded, frozen once projected). Then move the projection into #214, scoped to the shipped tool, with #215 extending it rather than inventing it.\n\nThat makes group 1 a deterministic test plus an implementation rather than an eval, and drops the model-credential dependency entirely.\n\nThe cost is real: #214 grows by roughly one group, and the projection gets designed against conversation rows before web-search payloads exist. My earlier argument against that still partly holds — but \"designed against the wrong payload\" is a weaker objection than \"the user can see data the assistant can't.\"",
