          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "That's a coherent and, I think, correct rule: **the model's view of a round should not degrade as the conversation moves past it.** It also resolves the inconsistency I flagged — no more \"load-bearing at step 2, disposable at turn N+1.\"\n\nThe failure-preservation part is the half people usually get wrong, and you're right to name it. If successes replay and failures don't, the model sees a history where every tool call worked. It will re-attempt something that was refused, or assume data it never got. A refused/cancelled/timed-out call carries real information — *this was tried, it did not produce a result* — and dropping it is worse than dropping a success.\n\nThat composes with the #293 settling work: it produces a durable, distinguishable cancellation record, which is exactly the thing that would need to replay as \"did not succeed.\"\n\n**Form — my recommendation, and the tradeoff:**\n\nProject into the existing `ModelMessage { role, content: string }` as fenced, labelled text rather than extending the context model to carry provider-native tool blocks. Text projection keeps provider portability (the whole reason that model is flattened), keeps untrusted MCP output inside a fence we control, and is a much smaller change. What it gives up is the structural call/result pairing providers can use for attention and cache segmentation — peers get that free because their message models already carry tool parts.\n\nWorth noting: the Anthropic-style \"every `tool_use` needs a matching `tool_result`\" constraint, which is the thing that makes structural replay fiddly, is now satisfiable *because* of #293 — every call gets settled. So the structural route is open later if the text projection measurably underperforms.\n\n**What this changes in the spec:**\n\n- The boundary requirement inverts. From \"raw results SHALL NOT be replayed\" to: tool observations SHALL survive into later turns, preserving outcome status, in a bounded, fenced, untrusted-label